  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 130 PM CDT Thu Sep 10 2026\n\nPretty quiet across the region today with surface high pressure\nin control. Temperatures as of 1 PM were in the lower to mid \n80s with notably less humidity than Wednesday in most areas. \nOtherwise, the main feature of note in the short term was a \nshortwave trough moving across OR/ID that will continue eastward\nacross WY and eventually spin up a surface low that moves \nthrough SD/MN on Friday and drags a cold front into the forecast\narea. While guidance is in good fairly good agreement that \nwe'll have a decent ribbon of instability ahead of the front, \nthere will also be quite a bit of capping to overcome. In \naddition, the strongest forcing with the wave looks to remain \njust north of our forecast area. As a result, CAMs are pretty \nsparse in storm coverage in our area, but a few still hint at \ndevelopment during the evening into overnight hours as moisture \ntransport ramps up a bit. Given modest shear, storm organization\nand strength should be somewhat limited, but can't completely \nrule out a strong to severe storm with damaging winds and/or \nhail being the primary threats. Latest forecast has a 20-30% \nchance of precip.\n\nThe forecast then gets a little tricky beyond that, with \nguidance differing on where that front stalls out/moves to \nSaturday into Sunday, along with timing and track of incoming \nshortwave energy. GEFS guidance generally suggests we'll see \nsome additional shower and storm development along the boundary\nin southeast NE/southwest IA as early as Saturday afternoon, \nbut moreso in the evening and overnight hours as shortwave \nenergy moves through and moisture transport points into the \narea. In contrast, EPS guidance is very limited in precip \ncoverage in that time period, with little to no forcing for \nascent and a more diffuse front. Heading into Sunday, that \nboundary starts to edge back northward with guidance in good \nagreement of moisture transport pointing more into our area. As \na result, Sunday is looking fairly wet, with 60-70% chances \nacross much of the area.\n\nWhile the weekend front will push farther north on Monday, \nanother cold front will approach from the northwest and lead to \nmore shower and storm chances. Ahead of that front, we should \nsee a decent amount of destabilization, though guidance does \nsuggest there could be lingering showers and storms early in the\nday, which may somewhat limit things. Shear will be on the \nincrease through the day, though the strongest deep layer shear \nremains post-frontal, but there could be just enough overlap in \nthis shear and instability ahead of the front to yield an \norganized severe weather threat. Obviously still lots of small-\nscale details to work out, but bottom line, Monday looks like \nour highest potential for severe weather over the next several \ndays.\n\nWe will stay in a wetter pattern into next week with a surface \nboundary hovering in or just south of the forecast area while \nseveral shortwaves slide through the zonal to slight \nsouthwesterly flow pattern aloft. Severe weather potential \nshould be somewhat limited after Monday with most of the \ninstability remaining to our south, though with repeated rounds \nof rain, can't rule out a flooding threat in a few spots. \nOtherwise, temperatures will remain warm through Friday (80s), \ncool down a bit for the weekend (mid 70s to 80), briefly warm \nback up Monday, then cool down for the remainder of the week \n(mid 60s to lower 70s). Of course, rain and cloud cover on any \ngiven day could throw a large wrench in the temperatures, so \nmake sure to keep an eye on the forecast.",
